      "id": 1,
      "text": "Portage Des Sioux earned its name when Sioux canoeists used the stretch between the Missouri and Mississippi Rivers as a portage route.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portage_Des_Sioux%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Treaties of Portage des Sioux signed in 1815 settled conflicts between Native Americans and the United States after the War of 1812.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portage_Des_Sioux%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Land Between the Rivers Historical Society, founded in 1978, runs a museum that showcases artifacts from the Mississippi–Missouri River region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://museumsdatabase.com/museums/view/17695",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Portage Des Sioux sits on the Mississippi River roughly opposite Elsah, Illinois, and hosts the riverside shrine of Our Lady of the Rivers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
